HST Office: Vietnamese .docx files from HST Writer came back from Word about 8 times slower to open.

New documents took their East Asian basic fonts from the CJK default of the platform (Noto Serif
CJK SC on Linux, NSimSun or SimSun on Windows) while the Latin fonts are Times New Roman. Word then
stores every precomposed Vietnamese letter of the Latin Extended Additional block (U+1E00-U+1EFF:
ế, ả, ứ, ệ ...) in a run of its own when it saves the document: a 400-paragraph plain Vietnamese
text grew from 400 to 50,830 runs and its document.xml from 0.26 MB to 1.47 MB, and a 300-citation
thesis from 4,146 to 204,224 runs. Measured with Word 16.0.15601: no split when the East Asian
font is Times New Roman, also with Arial as the Latin font of a run; split with Noto Serif CJK SC,
SimSun or Arial as the East Asian font, whatever the East Asian language and compatibility mode.

The East Asian basic fonts (Tools > Options > Writer > Basic Fonts (Asian)) default to the Latin
ones, Times New Roman. Documents without East Asian text look the same; East Asian text still gets
its glyphs through font fallback, and the options page can set another font.

Test: tests/runtime/check-defaults.py, tests/runtime/check-docx-word-interop.py (East Asian font of
a new document's .docx).
